        "summary":  "AWS explores architectural principles for managing agentic AI across heterogeneous enterprise environments, advocating for standardized control mechanisms ΓÇö identity and governance ΓÇö while preserving flexibility in execution. Three enterprise patterns are presented: internal agent platforms, customer-facing multi-tenant systems, and latency-optimized real-time applications.",
        "execSummary":  "The framing of enterprise patterns without vendor lock-in from an AWS blog is worth scrutinizing, but the underlying architectural guidance ΓÇö decouple governance from execution, standardize identity and observability, vary deployment patterns by latency and trust requirements ΓÇö is sound and largely transferable. For senior leaders building enterprise AI strategy, the three-pattern taxonomy maps cleanly onto the decision points most organizations face when scaling beyond a single pilot. The practical implication is that governance infrastructure should be designed before the agent fleet scales, not after. Note that the implementation examples lean heavily on Bedrock services, so without vendor lock-in is more aspirational than literal.",
